Node.js HTTP Module

1. What is the HTTP module?
    Node provides a built-in module called http.
    We don't install it:
        const http = require("node:http");
    It allows you to create an HTTP server directly.
    The basic flow is:
        Client
        │
        │ HTTP Request
        ▼
        Node.js HTTP Server
        │
        ├── Method
        ├── URL
        ├── Headers
        └── Body
        │
        ▼
        Your Logic
        │
        ▼
        HTTP Response
        │
        ├── Status Code
        ├── Headers
        └── Body
        ▼
        Client


2. Create your first HTTP server
    Create:
        src/server.js
            const http = require("node:http");

            const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
                res.end("Hello from Node.js");
            });

            server.listen(3000, () => {
                console.log("Server running on port 3000");
            });
    Run:
        node src/server.js
    Open:
        http://localhost:3000
    You'll get:
        Hello from Node.js


3. What is actually happening?
    This line:
        http.createServer()
    creates an HTTP server.

    This callback:
        (req, res) => {
            ...
        }
    is called whenever a request reaches the server.

    You can think of it as:
        Browser
        ↓
        GET /users
        ↓
        Node HTTP Server
        ↓
        (req, res) => {}

    The two important objects are:
        req
        res


4. req — Request object
    req contains information about the incoming HTTP request.
    For example:
        const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
            console.log(req.method);
            console.log(req.url);
        });
    If you visit:
        http://localhost:3000/users
    you'll get:
        GET
        /users


5. HTTP methods
    The most common methods are:
        GET
        POST
        PUT
        PATCH
        DELETE
    You can inspect the method:
        console.log(req.method);
    Example:
        GET
        For a POST request:    POST


6. req.url
    console.log(req.url);
    If the request is:
        GET /users
    you get:
        /users
    If:
        GET /users?page=2
    you get:
        /users?page=2
    This is where URL parsing becomes important.


7. Routing manually
    Without Express, you have to implement routing yourself.
        const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {

            if (req.method === "GET" && req.url === "/") {
                res.end("Home");
            }

            else if (req.method === "GET" && req.url === "/users") {
                res.end("Users");
            }

            else if (req.method === "GET" && req.url === "/products") {
                res.end("Products");
            }

            else {
                res.statusCode = 404;
                res.end("Not Found");
            }
        });
    Now:
        GET /   →    Home

    And:
        GET /users    →    Users

    This is essentially primitive routing.
    Express later gives us a much cleaner API:
        app.get("/users", handler);


8. HTTP status codes
    Don't just return:
        res.end("Not Found");
    Use an appropriate status code:
        res.statusCode = 404;
        res.end("Not Found");
    
    Common codes:
    | Status | Meaning               |
    | ------ | --------------------- |
    | 200    | OK                    |
    | 201    | Created               |
    | 204    | No Content            |
    | 400    | Bad Request           |
    | 401    | Unauthorized          |
    | 403    | Forbidden             |
    | 404    | Not Found             |
    | 409    | Conflict              |
    | 422    | Unprocessable Content |
    | 500    | Internal Server Error |

    For example:
        if (req.url === "/users") {
            res.statusCode = 200;
            res.end("Users");
        }


9. Response headers
    You can set headers:
        res.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/plain");
    Then:
        res.end("Hello");
    For JSON:
        res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");
    Then:
        res.end(JSON.stringify({
            message: "Hello",
            success: true
        }));
    The client receives JSON rather than plain text.


10. Build a JSON API
    Let's make a simple API:
        const http = require("node:http");
        const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {

            res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");

            if (req.method === "GET" && req.url === "/users") {

                res.statusCode = 200;

                res.end(JSON.stringify([
                    {
                        id: 1,
                        name: "John"
                    },
                    {
                        id: 2,
                        name: "Sarah"
                    }
                ]));

                return;
            }

            res.statusCode = 404;

            res.end(JSON.stringify({
                message: "Route not found"
            }));
        });

        server.listen(3000, () => {
            console.log("Server running on port 3000");
        });

    Now:
        GET /users
    returns:
        [
            {
                "id": 1,
                "name": "John"
            },
            {
                "id": 2,
                "name": "Sarah"
            }
        ]


11. Why return matters here
    Notice:
        res.end(...);
        return;
    Why?:
        Because after sending the response, you don't want the remaining routing logic to execute.
    For example:
        if (req.url === "/users") {
            res.end("Users");
        }

        res.statusCode = 404;
        res.end("Not Found");

    You could accidentally try to send multiple responses.
    That leads to errors such as:
        Error [ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT]
    So when manually routing, it's common to finish the request handler after responding.


12. Query parameters
    Suppose:
        /users?page=2&limit=10
    Instead of manually splitting strings, use Node's URL API.
        const { URL } = require("node:url");

        const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {

            const url = new URL(req.url, `http://${req.headers.host}`);

            console.log(url.pathname);
            console.log(url.searchParams.get("page"));
            console.log(url.searchParams.get("limit"));

            res.end("OK");
        });

    For:
        /users?page=2&limit=10

    you get:
        /users
        2
        10

    This is the proper conceptual separation:
        URL
        │
        ├── pathname
        │
        └── search parameters


13. Route parameters
    Now suppose:
        /users/123
    You want:
        userId = 123
    Without Express, you have to parse the path yourself.
    Conceptually:
        const url = new URL(req.url, `http://${req.headers.host}`);
        const parts = url.pathname.split("/");
        console.log(parts);
    For:
        /users/123
    you get approximately:
        ["", "users", "123"]
    Then:
        const userId = parts[2];

    This works, but becomes ugly very quickly.
    That's one reason frameworks such as Express are useful.


14. Request body — VERY important
    For:
        GET
    you usually don't have a meaningful request body.
    But for:
        POST
        PUT
        PATCH
    you commonly receive data in the body.
    Example request:
        {
            "name": "John",
            "email": "john@example.com"
        }
    Here's the important part:
        The request body arrives as a stream.


15. Reading a POST body
    Example:
        const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {

            if (req.method === "POST" && req.url === "/users") {

                let body = "";

                req.on("data", (chunk) => {
                    body += chunk;
                });

                req.on("end", () => {

                    const data = JSON.parse(body);

                    console.log(data);

                    res.statusCode = 201;
                    res.end("User created");
                });

                return;
            }

            res.statusCode = 404;
            res.end("Not Found");
        });


16. Why is req a stream?
    Because Node doesn't necessarily receive the entire request body at once.
    Imagine uploading:
        100 MB file
    You don't want Node to blindly load all 100 MB into memory before processing anything.
    Instead:
        Request
        ↓
        Chunk 1
        Chunk 2
        Chunk 3
        Chunk 4
        ...
        Chunk N
    The request is readable as a stream.
    That's why:
        req.on("data", ...)
    and:
        req.on("end", ...)
    exist.


17. res.end() is important
    You must eventually finish the response.
        res.end("Hello");
    This:
        writes the response body
        signals that the response is complete
    You can also do:
        res.write("Hello ");
        res.write("World");
        res.end();
    Response:
        Hello World
    This demonstrates that HTTP responses can also be streamed.


18. res.write() vs res.end()
    res.write():
        Writes a chunk:
            res.write("Hello");
            The response isn't finished yet.
    res.end():
        Finishes the response:
        res.end("Done");

    or:
        res.write("Hello");
        res.end();
    Think:
        write()
        write()
        write()
        ↓
        end()


19. Server lifecycle
    When you do:
        server.listen(3000);
    Node starts listening for incoming connections.
    You can also handle server events:
        server.on("listening", () => {
            console.log("Server started");
        });

        server.on("error", (error) => {
            console.error(error);
        });

    And gracefully stop:
        server.close(() => {
            console.log("Server closed");
        });

    Graceful shutdown becomes very important in production.


20. Practical API
    const http = require("node:http");
    const { URL } = require("node:url");

    const users = [
        { id: 1, name: "John" },
        { id: 2, name: "Sarah" }
    ];

    const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {

        const url = new URL(
            req.url,
            `http://${req.headers.host}`
        );

        res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");

        // GET /users
        if (req.method === "GET" && url.pathname === "/users") {
            res.statusCode = 200;
            res.end(JSON.stringify(users));
            return;
        }

        // GET /users/:id
        if (
            req.method === "GET" &&
            url.pathname.startsWith("/users/")
        ) {
            const id = Number(url.pathname.split("/")[2]);

            const user = users.find(user => user.id === id);

            if (!user) {
                res.statusCode = 404;

                res.end(JSON.stringify({
                    message: "User not found"
                }));

                return;
            }

            res.statusCode = 200;
            res.end(JSON.stringify(user));
            return;
        }

        res.statusCode = 404;

        res.end(JSON.stringify({
            message: "Route not found"
        }));
    });

    server.listen(3000, () => {
        console.log("Server running on port 3000");
    });


21. Why do we need Express then?
    Even for two routes, we're manually handling:
        URL parsing
        Routing
        Methods
        Status codes
        Headers
        Request body
        Responses
        Errors
    Imagine 50 routes.
        code becomes:
            if
            else if
            else if
            else if
            else if
            ...
    Express gives you:
        app.get("/users", getUsers);
        app.get("/users/:id", getUser);
        app.post("/users", createUser);
        app.put("/users/:id", updateUser);
        app.delete("/users/:id", deleteUser);
    Much cleaner.


22. Is Express a replacement for Node.js?
    No.
    Express is a web framework that runs on Node.js.
    Conceptually:
        JavaScript
            ↓
        Node.js
            ↓
        HTTP capabilities
            ↓
        Express
            ↓
        Your API
    Express uses Node's HTTP infrastructure underneath.


23. Build this without Express:
    GET    /users
    GET    /users/:id
    POST   /users
    DELETE /users/:id

    Use an in-memory array:
        const users = [];
    For POST:
        {
            "name": "Dharitri",
            "email": "dharitri@example.com"
        }

    Return:
        201 Created
    For a missing user:
        404 Not Found


24. Interview questions
    Q1. How do you create an HTTP server in Node.js?
        Using the built-in http module and http.createServer().

    Q2. What are req and res?
        req represents the incoming HTTP request and contains information such as the method, URL, headers and body. res is used to construct and send the HTTP response.

    Q3. How do you set a response status code?
        res.statusCode = 404;

    Q4. How do you send JSON using the HTTP module?
        res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");

        res.end(JSON.stringify({
            message: "Success"
        }));

    Q5. What is res.end()?
        It signals that the HTTP response is complete and optionally sends the final response data.

    Q6. How does Node receive a POST request body?
        The incoming request is a readable stream, so the body can arrive in chunks. We can listen for data events and process the complete body on the end event.

    Q7. Why does Node expose the request body as a stream?
        It allows Node to process potentially large data incrementally instead of requiring the entire body to be loaded into memory at once.

    Q8. Why use Express if Node already has the HTTP module?
        Node's HTTP module provides low-level HTTP functionality, but Express provides higher-level abstractions such as routing, middleware, request parsing and response helpers, making API development significantly easier to structure and maintain.



                HTTP Request
                     │
                     ▼
              Node HTTP Server
                     │
             ┌───────┴───────┐
             │               │
          req object      res object
             │               │
       method/url/body    status/headers
             │               │
             ▼               ▼
             Your Application Logic
                     │
                     ▼
                res.end()
                     │
                     ▼
                HTTP Response
